cartridge "TREASURE WHEEL/BOB BLACKMER/1986"

' TREASURE WHEEL
' A ColecoVision game based on "Treasure Wheel" by Bob Blackmer, Ahoy!
' February 1986 (Commodore 64, BASIC with a little machine code; disk
' ahoy1986-02). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic),
' 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-for-line port: the rules are the listing's (orig/), the game
' is rebuilt for the ColecoVision: a wooden wheel with a turning hand,
' gold plates over the hidden letters, a joystick letter panel, sounds and
' a title tune.
'
' The game: a hidden name, place, thing, phrase or title is shown as gold
' plates on the board, its category below. On your turn, SPIN the wheel or
' SOLVE the puzzle. The wheel has eight spaces: 100, 1000, TILT, 400, 200,
' 500, LOSE A TURN and 300. On a value, pick a letter: every time it is in
' the puzzle you score that value, and you keep the turn; a letter that is
' not there passes the turn. TILT wipes your score for this puzzle and
' passes the turn; LOSE A TURN passes it. Solve the puzzle (or show its
' last letter) for 100 more and the win. The winner of each puzzle adds
' its score to a running total; a set is a number of puzzles, and the
' player with the most puzzles won (then the most points) takes it.
'
' From the original (orig/treasure-wheel.bas): the wheel's eight values in
' the same order (DATA line 80); a letter found scores the value for each
' time it appears and keeps the turn (lines 770-790); a miss, TILT (score
' to 0) and LOSE A TURN pass it (660-690); a letter already used is
' refused (1000-1040); solving adds 100 and wins (870), a wrong answer
' passes the turn (880-890); showing the last letter adds 100 and wins
' (1230-1250); games won and total points are kept (1280-1290); a set is
' ten puzzles (1340); the 100 puzzles in five categories of 20 (lines
' 120-430), category then puzzle picked at random.
'
' Liberties: joystick instead of keys: SPIN / SOLVE are chosen with the
' stick, letters on a 26-letter panel (used letters are dimmed); to solve,
' you fill the hidden letters one by one from the panel, and the first
' wrong letter is a wrong answer (the C64 typed the whole answer); the
' wheel is a ring with a turning hand and a running gem instead of the
' C64's two flipping sprites, landing on each space one time in eight; a
' set is 3, 5 or 10 puzzles (the C64: 10) and no puzzle comes twice in a
' set; one player against the computer, or two players with a controller
' each (the C64 was two players on one keyboard); the computer opponent
' picks common letters first (with a few odd choices) and solves when
' three quarters of the letters are showing or only two letters are
' missing; puzzles wrap on three lines of 20; the C64's "!!WAY TO GO!!"
' became a results page. The puzzle list is the original's (its people,
' places and titles are of 1986).
'
' Controls: joystick only. Left / right choose SPIN or SOLVE, a button
' confirms. On the letter panel the stick moves the box, a button picks.
' Player 1 uses controller 1, player 2 controller 2; against the computer,
' either controller. On the title, up / down choose a line, left / right
' or a button change it, a button on START starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` after every `clear
' sprites`. Own xorshift random numbers.
'
' Workarounds for Amy bugs twheel-fill-local-array removed 2026-11-01.

